---
// Lesson 7.4 — Ladders II: Mounts, Tops & Polish
// The top-of-ladder problem, mount prompts, climb polish.
import Lesson from '../../layouts/LessonLayout.astro';

const body = `<h1>7.4 — Ladders II: Mounts, Tops &amp; Polish</h1>

<p class="lesson-lead">
    Your ladder works in the middle — and fails at both ends. Today:
    the top-of-ladder problem (the hardest 3 tiles in platformer
    engineering), the mount prompt, and the polish pass that makes
    climbing feel like an answer to a question the level asked.
</p>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Concept Lab</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~15 min</span>
    </div>

    <p><strong>The top-of-ladder problem.</strong> Climb to the top
    of yesterday's ladder (<a href="7.3.html">7.3</a>) and keep
    holding W. You rise INTO the floor above (or hover at the seam,
    depending on your geometry). Every game with ladders has solved
    this; the three classic answers:</p>
    <table>
        <tr><th>Solution</th><th>Feel</th><th>Cost</th></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Stop-at-top:</strong> when grounded ABOVE the ladder top, exit climb state</td><td>Honest, sudden</td><td>Needs a check: raycast down when climbing past the top</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Pop-off:</strong> climbing W past the top applies a small hop</td><td>Flows into a jump</td><td>Physics fiddly — can overshoot into enemies</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Platform top:</strong> a solid tile caps the ladder; you stop under it</td><td>Cheap and common</td><td>You must JUMP the last bit — level design must allow it</td></tr>
    </table>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Socratic</h3>
        <p>
            The starter's levels use the third answer mostly — capped
            ladders. Why is the CHEAPEST solution often the most
            honest one? What does the player learn about your game's
            rules from a capped ladder vs. a magic pop-off?
        </p>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Build Lab</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~25 min</span>
    </div>

    <p><strong>Step 1 — implement stop-at-top.</strong> In the climb
    branch, when W is held and your ground raycast (you already own
    it from <a href="6.2.html">6.2</a> — extend its reach) finds
    solid ground at your feet while climbing, exit the climb state
    cleanly:</p>
    <pre><code>// while climbing, if there is floor under my feet at the top:
if (Physics2D.Raycast(transform.position, Vector2.down,
        groundCheckDistance + 0.6f, groundLayer))
{
    isOnLadder = false;
    rb.gravityScale = 2;
    // snap onto the ledge: tiny translate toward it, or leave
    // it to physics — play with both, choose one
}</code></pre>

    <p><strong>Step 2 — the mount prompt.</strong> The middle of a
    ladder works by walking into it — but the BOTTOM of a tall
    ladder often sits above the player's reach. Add a one-liner
    prompt (same UI family as your HUD thinking):
    when within range of a ladder's base, show "Press W to climb"
    text for exactly as long as the condition holds. Cheap version:
    a trigger zone at the base toggling a world-space TextMesh
    visible/hidden. The rule: prompts die the instant they are
    obeyed or no longer relevant.</p>

    <p><strong>Step 3 — polish passes (pick two).</strong>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>Animation:</strong> add a Climb parameter to the
            animator; drive it from isOnLadder, and let climbing
            idle vs. climbing move show different frames</li>
        <li><strong>Sound:</strong> a soft tick per climb-step (your
            feet know the ladder's rung count — or just a
            heartbeat)</li>
        <li><strong>Ladder edges:</strong> at the bottom of the climb
            state, restore a tiny gravity moment so you do not
            hang in the air when the trigger ends</li>
        <li><strong>Ladders through platforms:</strong> your
            one-way platform tinker from
            <a href="7.2.html">7.2</a> lets a climb pass THROUGH
            the cap — the two features love each other</li>
    </ul></p>

    <div class="bug-hunt">
        <h3>Bug Hunt: Endgame of ends</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>At the top, you exit climb WHILE holding W, grounded
                on the ledge. But your code above still runs the
                translate BEFORE the check. What happens on the
                frame after the exit — one-frame drift INTO the
                ledge? Fix the order, then explain why one frame of
                a 60fps game still matters.</li>
            <li>The prompt appears when you can reach the ladder
                BASE, but you jump UP to the trigger from below.
                Should the prompt appear mid-jump? Why is "prompt
                when possible" the wrong rule — what IS the right
                one?</li>
            <li>Two ladders back-to-back (a ladder shaft). The exit
                trigger of ladder 1 fires at the seam... does your
                enter of ladder 2 fire before the exit? Draw the
                two messages on a timeline; who wins?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Tinker</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~10 min</span>
    </div>

    <div class="tinker">
        <h3>Tinker: Ladder levels</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Design a "chimney": two ladders 3 tiles apart with
                enemies patrolling the top. What is the player
                reading during the climb — and why is climbing a
                PERFECT vulnerability window (you cannot jump
                away mid-climb)?</li>
            <li>Make the ladder the SAFE route and the jump route the
                fast-but-spikey one. Which do testers choose —
                record it, this is <a href="9.5.html">9.5</a>
                data in the wild.</li>
            <li>Commit + push: <code>"7.4a: ladder tops + mount prompt + polish"</code>.</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="checklist">
        <h3>Checkpoint — before moving on, you must be able to:</h3>
        <ul>
            <li>Explain the top-of-ladder problem and implement stop-at-top with a raycast</li>
            <li>Build a mount prompt that dies when obeyed</li>
            <li>Drive climb animation from state you already track</li>
            <li>Reason about order-of-operations on the exit frame</li>
        </ul>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="resources">
    <h3>Reference Docs</h3>
    <p>Need a deeper explanation? These pages cover the same concepts:</p>
    <ul>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-07/index.html">Unit 7: Level Building &amp; Ladders</a> — the polish reference</li>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-06/index.html">Unit 6: Jumping</a> — the ground raycast you are reusing</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Next: <a href="7.5.html">7.5 — Design a Big Cave: Flow &amp; Difficulty</a></p>
</div>`;
